---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../../consts';
---

<PageLayout
	title={`About ${SITE_TITLE}`}
	description="What UniBudget is, who it is for, and how this static blog is built and deployed."
>
	<h1>About {SITE_TITLE}</h1>
	<p class="lead">
		UniBudget is a budgeting tool built around the way student money actually behaves: a few
		large, predictable costs, a long tail of small ones, and income that arrives in irregular
		amounts.
	</p>

	<section>
		<h2>What it is</h2>
		<p>
			The application records your income and expense transactions, keeps a budget for the
			current month, and shows overall spending against that budget. Each account only ever sees
			its own transactions and budgets. There are no bank connections, no card details and no
			imports from a bank statement — you enter what you spend, which is usually the point at
			which you start noticing it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Who it is for</h2>
		<p>
			Students who want a short weekly routine rather than a full accounting package. If your
			income is a maintenance loan, a part-time job, a scholarship, help from family, or some
			combination of those, the useful question is the same one: how much of this month's money
			is already spoken for?
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>What this blog covers</h2>
		<p>
			The writing here is about the habits around the tool: putting a realistic monthly budget
			together, finding the small recurring costs that quietly add up, planning for bills that
			land all at once, and setting money aside without pretending that a student income is
			stable. Every article is general information, not personalised financial, tax or
			investment advice.
		</p>
		<p><a class="inline-link" href="/blog/">Read the articles →</a></p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>How this site is built</h2>
		<p>
			This blog is a static Astro site. The pages are generated ahead of time as plain HTML and
			CSS, stored in an S3 bucket that is not publicly readable, and served through CloudFront
			over HTTPS. There is no application server behind these pages, which is why they load
			quickly and cannot leak data from the budgeting app.
		</p>
		<p>
			The budgeting application itself is a separate, server-rendered Astro deployment with its
			own database and login. The two deployments share component and content source code in one
			repository but publish completely different artifacts: the blog never includes API routes,
			sessions or dashboard pages.
		</p>
		<p>
			UniBudget is a CS4421 (DevOps) project. The static deployment is reproducible from the
			repository, and the deployment workflow builds the static configuration only, verifies the
			expected HTML files, uploads without deleting unrelated objects, and waits for the
			CloudFront invalidation before checking the live pages.
		</p>
	</section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	h1 {
		margin-bottom: 0.4em;
		font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 6vw, 2.8em);
	}
	.lead {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-size: 1.15em;
	}
	section + section {
		margin-top: 2em;
	}
	h2 {
		font-size: 1.55em;
	}
	.inline-link {
		font-weight: 600;
		text-decoration: none;
	}
</style>
